<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nyatapola Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1702</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nyatapola is a five-storeyed pagoda temple in Taumadhi Square, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>. Built by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhupatindra-malla">Bhupatindra Malla</a> in 1702, it stands about 30 metres high on a five-tiered plinth flanked by pairs of guardian figures, and is the tallest temple in the valley. It survived the earthquakes of 1934 and 2015 with little damage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nyatapola-temple.jpg" alt="Nyatapola Temple" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Fuhrmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kathmandu_und_Bhaktapur_2013-05-11_16-03-39.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
